  "text_pages": [
    "Accessibility and Support Options\nSkip to main content\nStudents\nitslearning\nAccess to your courses\nmySDU\nFor students at SDU\nSDUmail - Webmail service\nOutlook Web Mail\nSDU Student Self-Service\nCourse registration, exams and results\nLibrary\nSee your status, reservations and renew loans\nDigitalExam\nLogin to DigitalExam\nEmployees\nitslearning\nSDU's e-learn platform\nSDUNet.dk\nSDU's intranet\nDigitalExam\nLogin to DigitalExam\nFind\nFind person\nSearch for contact information on employees\nDirectory\nFinding your way at the University of Southern Denmark\nSpecial Needs Support\nSDU is committed to giving all students the best possible offset for an amazing exchange experience and to creating an inclusive and supportive environment for all students.\nGet an overview of your support options before and during your exchange at SDU as a student with physical, mental or health-related conditions, which is collectively denoted “special needs”.\nPrepare your stay\nDocumentation and Preparation\nIf you have special needs and you are planning your exchange at SDU, it is important that you reach out to us as soon as possible, so we can help you prepare for a successful stay.\nWe cannot guarantee to offer the requested support, but the earlier we get information, the more time we have to guide you in the right direction and hopefully offer a suitable solution to your needs.\nWe recommend that you:\nInform us of your special needs as early as possible\nCoordinate with your home university’s international office for additional support\nPrepare relevant documentation from your home university and/or healthcare provider\nIf you have any questions before you apply, please feel free to reach out to\nincoming@sdu.dk\n.\nAcademic Support\nExchange students are not eligible for the full range of Special Educational Support (SPS) available to degree-seeking students, but we will do our best to accommodate your needs.\nPossible support options include:\nExtended time for exams\nAccessible course materials\nFacilitation of contact for external service providers\nWho to contact when?\nSDU International Office:\nFunctions as a facilitator to help you get in contact with the relevant entity at SDU.\nCannot pre-assess documentation or make decisions regarding academic support.\nThe Faculty / Study Board:\nProcesses applications for special exam conditions.\nAs an exchange/guest student, your courses may be offered by more than one study board. If so, you must send an application to all relevant study boards.\nPlease include documentation of your disability in the application.\nAll decisions regarding academic support are made by the faculty based on the application. Specific outcomes are not guaranteed.\nFinancial Support and Funding\nSDU does not offer financial support or funding for incoming exchange students. If you require any aid, assistance, or support services for your stay, you must bring this/these with you from your home country, as SDU does not provide these resources directly.\nWe strongly encourage you to contact your home university and relevant support organizations in your home country before your arrival to ensure you have everything you need during your exchange.\nErasmus+ Funding: Fewer Opportunities\nA key aspect of the Erasmus Scholarship is its dedication to ensuring that everyone has equal access to educational development opportunities. This means that if you are an Erasmus+ exchange student,  you could be eligible for additional grants to help cover some of your travel, accommodation, or living expenses while studying abroad.\nNote:\nSDU does not fund or provide grants for incoming students. Potential grants are provided by your home university. To find out if you are eligible for any (additional) grants, please reach out to the international office at your home university.\nInsurance Coverage\nSDU does not provide insurance coverage for students. It is your responsibility to ensure that you are adequately insured throughout your stay.\nOnce you have received your Danish CPR number (you will get information about this after your admission to SDU), you will be covered by the\nDanish public health insurance system\n.\nIn addition, with a documented disability you might be entitled to receive relevant assistance from the municipality. SDU does not fund or cover any costs in relation to external support. You must reach out to the relevant municipality yourself to find out about your options.\nHealth insurance\nUntil you have received your Danish CPR number and are covered by the Danish public health care system, it is your responsibility to secure adequate health insurance from another provider to cover any medical needs. In addition, it is entirely your responsibility to make sure that you are sufficiently covered throughout your stay.\nAre you an EU citizen or a citizen in one of the countries of Norway, Iceland, Liechtenstein, Switzerland or the UK?\nWe recommend that you get your blue\nEuropean health insurance card\nbefore traveling to Denmark.\nAdditional Insurance Coverage\nYou are strongly advised to obtain:\nLiability insurance\nAccident insurance\nHome insurance\nThese can typically be arranged through Danish banks or insurance companies.\nMotor Vehicle Insurance\nIf you bring a motor vehicle to Denmark, you:\nmust have valid insurance for the vehicle\nare required to register the vehicle with\nThe Danish Motor Vehicle Agency\nHousing\nEasy-access student housing may be available upon request. Please indicate your special needs in your housing application or contact SDU Accommodation Office for further assistance.\nContact SDU Accommodation Office\nCounselling and Study Support\nAt SDU, we want you to succeed in adjusting to a new environment, and there are plenty of opportunities for support during your stay if you need it.\nA good place to start would be our counselling and study support team. Whether you have personal or academic uncertainties or just want to have a talk about adjusting to student life in a new country, you can get support during your stay.\nTransport and Accessibility\nCampus Accessibility\nAll SDU’s campuses are equipped with elevators, accessible entrances, and designated parking spaces.\nGetting to and around SDU’s campuses is generally straightforward, and there are several accessible options available.\nCampus Odense has its own tram stop, “SDU University”, right outside the main entrance.\nCampus Sønderborg is located right next to the train station “Sønderborg Station”.\nFind your way at SDU\nTravelling to Denmark\nAirports\nCopenhagen Airport (CPH)\nTrains depart frequently from Terminal 3 connecting to all SDU’s campus cities.\nDirect trains to Odense\nConnecting flight to Sønderborg Airport (small airport)\nBillund Airport (BLL)\nTravel to/from SDU’s campuses involves a combination of bus and train.\nSønderborg Airport (SGD)\nSmall airport mostly used for domestic flights to/from CPH Airport. Only relevant to students at Campus Sønderborg.\nTrain\nThe train service provider DSB connects most of Denmark and Europe, and you can go to/from all SDU’s campus cities with DSB.\nIf you depart from Europe, you might be able to plan your entire trip with DSB.\nRead more and plan your journey with DSB\nDSB also offers\ninformation on accessibility and support for people with special needs\n. The page is currently only in Danish, but you can use an online translation tool to translate the information.\nBus\nThe bus service provider Flixbus connects most of Denmark, and you can go to/from all SDU’s campus cities except Esbjerg with Flixbus.\nIf you depart from Europe, you might be able to plan your entire trip with Flixbus.\nRead more and plan your journey with Flixbus\n.\nFlixbus also offers\ninformation on accessibility and support for people with special needs\n.\nGetting Around in Denmark\nIn general, public transport in Denmark is easily accessible with level access or ramps. Use\nwww.rejseplanen.dk\n(Journey Planner) to plan your trip – (possibility of ticking an accessibility search option).\nIf you are unsure whether you can bring your wheelchair or any other facility on a bus, train or metro, you can call “Your Public Transport” on +45 7015 7000.\nLimitations\nSDU cannot pre‑assess documentation\nSDU cannot guarantee academic aids\nDecisions on academic support are made by the faculties\nSDU does not offer financial support\nSDU does not fund external assistance\nShare on\nFacebook\nX\nLinkedin\nEmail\nCopy link\nInternational Office\r\nUniversity of Southern Denmark\nCampusvej 55\nOdense M - DK-5230\nPhone: +45 6550 2264\nSend E-mail\nSee Map\nLast Updated 10.03.2026"
